WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:08.870
In the last two videos we have been looking
at some of the advantages of the

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:14.280
fact that ‘electric current heats up objects
through which it flows’.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.360
Electric water heaters, light bulbs and electric
iron are few of the electric

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.509
appliances that make use of this fact.

00:00:22.509 --> 00:00:25.830
However it has its drawbacks too.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.320
For
instance if these appliances are overheated

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:33.309
then they may get damaged or
even catch fire!

00:00:33.309 --> 00:00:38.469
In fact not only these appliances, every appliance
in our house is affected by

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:41.059
the unwanted high electric currents.

00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:47.250
There is a limit to the amount of electric
current that any device can withstand.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:51.879
If the current exceeds that limit, the
components of the device heat up and this

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:54.550
may even lead to fire!

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.280
So is there
any way to prevent that from happening?

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.949
There is a device which is used to prevent
this and it is called ‘Electric fuse’.

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:05.820
Well it comes in many shapes and sizes.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.670
This is what it generally looks like.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.290
It
has a fuse wire and it is connected to the

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:15.080
two terminals which are made of
some metal.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.660
This wire is made up of zinc, copper, silver
or aluminium.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:24.730
The rest of the switch is basically made of
a non-combustible material.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:27.010
And it
makes perfect sense too.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.150
We should make sure that the device which
is used

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:33.600
to prevent fires in other devices must not
catch fire!

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.840
Now how does it prevent household electrical
appliances from catching fire?

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.630
Well this fuse is introduced in EVERY circuit
in our household.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.670
This is how the
basic circuit will look like.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:50.120
We have seen this simplified circuit in our
videos.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:56.310
Ok, so when the current flows through the
circuit, it heats up the fuse wire.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:00.680
And when there is heavy current in the circuit,
the wire simply melts resulting

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.920
in the broken circuit!

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.780
Yes the circuit breaks and the current does
not reach the

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:07.780
appliance.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:11.140
So this is how an electric fuse functions!

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.970
Simple isn’t it?

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:17.650
Now there is one obvious disadvantage of electric
fuse that you must have

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:18.650
noticed!

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:22.749
Once the fuse wire melts, it cannot reform
itself.

00:02:22.749 --> 00:02:26.810
We have to replace
the fuse with the new one manually!

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:31.099
To overcome this problem, automatic fuses
are used nowadays.

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:35.129
They are also
called ‘Miniature circuit breakers’ often

00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:38.329
abbreviated as MCB.

00:02:38.329 --> 00:02:40.980
They work like automatic switches.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.510
Whenever there is excess amount of
current flow in the circuit, they automatically

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.909
‘switch off’.

00:02:46.909 --> 00:02:50.040
This breaks the
circuit and the appliances are saved from

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.930
excessive current.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.850
They also
automatically get switched on when the flow

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:55.829
is back to normal!

